{"answer":"To group chip design manual snippets into logical blocks for better technical QA, the most directly relevant approach is to reorganize evidence around the functional units that correspond to how queries “want” information, rather than treating snippets as independent chunks. In EDA documentation QA, one method replaces chunk-only retrieval with typed “functional units” (recorded as hyperedges linking source chunks) and trains an encoder to align queries with these units, improving retrieval-to-evidence matching for answer generation [6].

If your manual is HTML-like and already uses internal links, you can also group snippets by leveraging hyperlink structure during retrieval, encoding hyperlink relations as metadata and performing graph-like retrieval of locally relevant content [2]. For general chunking, you can choose or learn boundaries adaptively using intrinsic, document-based metrics, including “block integrity” and “contextual coherence,” so blocks better preserve the document’s structure for RAG [3].

These differ in mechanism: [6] targets EDA by functional evidence units; [2] exploits hyperlink topology; [3] optimizes chunk boundaries via intrinsic metrics. If you need domain-specific “chip” semantics beyond EDA, none of the provided papers explicitly covers chip manual block definitions beyond these general retrieval/chunking strategies [6,2,3].","grounded":true}
